window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_botany_molecularbasisofinheritance=[
{"id":"neet-2026-q104","pn":104,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","st":"Transcription unit","q":"Which of the following statements are correct with reference to a transcription unit?\nA. A transcription unit in DNA is defined primarily by three regions : promoter, structural gene and terminator.\nB. The promoter is said to be located towards the 5′-end of the structural gene.\nC. The promoter is a DNA sequence that provides binding site for RNA polymerase.\nD. The promoter defines the template and coding strands.\nE. The terminator is located towards the 3′-end of the coding strand and it defines the end of the process of transcription.","o":["A, B, C, D and E","B, C, D and E only","A, C, D and E only","A, B, C and D only"],"c":0,"s":"The promoter and terminator flank the structural gene in a transcription unit.\nThe promoter is located towards the 5′-end of the structural gene.\nIt is a DNA sequence that gives the binding site for RNA polymerase, and its presence also defines the template and coding strands.\nThe terminator is located towards the 3′-end of the coding strand and usually defines the end of transcription.\nSo all of A, B, C, D and E are correct."},
{"id":"neet-2026-q116","pn":116,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","st":"DNA fingerprinting","q":"Arrange the following steps of DNA fingerprinting in a correct sequence.\nA. Isolation of DNA and its digestion by restriction endonucleases.\nB. Hybridisation using a labelled VNTR probe.\nC. Transferring of separated DNA fragments to synthetic membranes.\nD. Detection of hybridised DNA fragments by autoradiography.\nE. Separation of DNA fragments by electrophoresis.\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","o":["A, E, C, B, D","A, E, B, C, D","A, B, D, C, E","A, D, B, E, C"],"c":0,"s":"Correct order of DNA fingerprinting:\nA. Isolate DNA and cut it with restriction endonuclease.\nE. Separate the fragments by size using gel electrophoresis.\nC. Transfer the separated fragments to synthetic membranes of nylon or nitrocellulose.\nB. Let probes complementary to the VNTR locus hybridise with the fragments.\nD. Finally detect the hybridised fragments under X-rays by autoradiography.\nSo A, E, C, B, D."},
{"id":"neet-2026-q123","pn":123,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","st":"Packaging of DNA helix","q":"Which of the following statements are correct with reference to packaging of DNA helix?\nA. Histones are organized to form a unit of eight molecules called histone octamer.\nB. Histones are negatively charged basic proteins.\nC. Histones are rich in the basic amino acid residues - lysine and arginine.\nD. The positively charged DNA is wrapped around the histone octamer to form nucleosome.\nE. The packaging of chromatin at higher levels requires an additional set of proteins called non-histone chromosomal proteins.\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","o":["A, B and D only","A, C and E only","C, D and E only","B, D and E only"],"c":1,"s":"Eight histones combine to form a histone octamer.\nHistones are positively charged basic proteins, rich in lysine and arginine.\nThe negatively charged DNA is wrapped around the histone octamer to form a nucleosome.\nPackaging of chromatin at higher levels needs NHC (non-histone chromosomal) proteins.\nSo A, C and E are correct; B and D are wrong."},
{"id":"neet-2026-q125","pn":125,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","st":"Lac operon","q":"In the lac operon, the z gene codes for","o":["transacetylase","the repressor of lac operon","permease","beta-galactosidase"],"c":3,"s":"In the lac operon:\ni gene codes for the regulator (repressor) protein.\nz gene codes for beta-galactosidase.\ny gene codes for permease.\na gene codes for transacetylase."},
{"id":"neet-2026r-q163","pn":163,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","st":"Lac operon","q":"Which of the following statements about lac-operon is correct?","o":["Lactose activates repressor to bind to the operator","Genes i, z, y and a share single common promoter","Galactose can act as an inducer of lac operon","Gene i is constitutively expressed"],"c":3,"s":"Galactose cannot act as an inducer of lac operon.\nGene i is constitutively expressed in lac operon.\nThe repressor binds to the operator when lactose is not available.\nGenes z, y and a are structural genes and have a different promoter from that of gene i."},
{"id":"neet-2026r-q180","pn":180,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","st":"Transcription","q":"Which of the following enzymes synthesizes precursor mRNA?","o":["RNA polymerase II","RNA polymerase III","DNA polymerase","RNA polymerase I"],"c":0,"s":"RNA polymerase II transcribes the precursor of mRNA, the heterogeneous nuclear RNA (hnRNA)."},
{"id":"neet-2025-q106","pn":106,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","st":"Human genome","q":"Which chromosome in the human genome has the highest number of genes?","o":["Chromosome X","Chromosome Y","Chromosome 1","Chromosome 10"],"c":2,"s":"In the human genome, chromosome 1 has the highest number of genes, i.e., 2968."},
{"id":"neet-2025-q114","pn":114,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","st":"RNA types and RNAi","q":"Given below are two statements :\nStatement I : Transfer RNAs and ribosomal RNA do not interact with mRNA.\nStatement II : RNA interference (RNAi) takes place in all eukaryotic organisms as a method of cellular defence.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :","o":["Both statement I and statement II are correct","Both statement I and statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but statement II is correct"],"c":3,"s":"Both transfer RNAs and ribosomal RNA interact with mRNA, so Statement I is incorrect.\nRNA interference (RNAi) takes place in all eukaryotic organisms as a method of cellular defence, so Statement II is correct."},
{"id":"neet-2025-q129","pn":129,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","st":"Transcription and RNA processing","q":"Which of the following are the post-transcriptional events in an eukaryotic cell?\nA. Transport of pre-mRNA to cytoplasm prior to splicing.\nB. Removal of introns and joining of exons.\nC. Addition of methyl group at 5' end of hnRNA.\nD. Addition of adenine residues at 3' end of hnRNA.\nE. Base pairing of two complementary RNAs.\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","o":["A, B, C only","B, C, D only","B, C, E only","C, D, E only"],"c":1,"s":"Transcription is copying of genetic information from one strand of DNA into RNA.\nTransport of pre-mRNA to cytoplasm prior to splicing is not a post-transcriptional event.\nThe primary transcript becomes functional mRNA after post-transcriptional processing in 3 steps:\nModification of 5' end by capping.\nTailing.\nSplicing.\nBase pairing of two complementary RNAs is not a post-transcriptional event.\nHence B, C, D are post-transcriptional events in a eukaryotic cell."},
{"id":"neet-2025-q134","pn":134,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","st":"DNA as genetic material","q":"Match List I with List II:\nA. Alfred Hershey and Martha Chase — I. Streptococcus pneumoniae\nB. Euchromatin — II. Densely packed and dark-stained\nC. Frederick Griffith — III. Loosely packed and light-stained\nD. Heterochromatin — IV. DNA as genetic material confirmation\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","o":["A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I"],"c":2,"s":"Proof that DNA is the genetic material came from the experiment of Alfred Hershey and Martha Chase (A-IV).\nEuchromatin is the lightly stained region with loosely packed chromatin fibre (B-III).\nFrederick Griffith did experiments with different strains of Streptococcus pneumoniae (C-I).\nHeterochromatin is the darkly stained region with tightly packed chromatin fibre (D-II)."},
{"id":"neet-2025-q163","pn":163,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","st":"Genetic code","q":"Who proposed that the genetic code for amino acids should be made up of three nucleotides?","o":["George Gamow","Francis Crick","Jacque Monod","Franklin Stahl"],"c":0,"s":"George Gamow, a physicist, proposed that the genetic code for amino acids should be made up of three nucleotides."},
{"id":"neet-2025-q174","pn":174,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","st":"Transcription","q":"Which factor is important for termination of transcription?","o":["α (alpha)","σ (sigma)","ρ (rho)","γ (gamma)"],"c":2,"s":"In prokaryotes the RNA polymerase can only catalyse elongation.\nIt associates for a short time with the initiation factor (σ) and the termination factor (ρ), to start and to end transcription respectively."},
{"id":"neet-2024-q125","pn":125,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","st":"Transcription unit","q":"A transcription unit in DNA is defined primarily by the three regions in DNA and these are with respect to upstream and down stream end;","o":["Inducer, Repressor, Structural gene","Promotor, Structural gene, Terminator","Repressor, Operator gene, Structural gene","Structural gene, Transposons, Operator gene"],"c":1,"s":"A transcription unit in DNA is defined by three regions:\n(i) a promoter,\n(ii) the structural gene,\n(iii) a terminator.\nThe promoter lies towards the 5' end (upstream) of the structural gene, with reference to the coding strand.\nThe terminator lies towards the 3' end (downstream) of the coding strand."},
{"id":"neet-2024-q129","pn":129,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","st":"Lac operon","q":"The lactose present in the growth medium of bacteria is transported to the cell by the action of","o":["Permease","Polymerase","Beta-galactosidase","Acetylase"],"c":0,"s":"The y gene of the lac operon codes for permease.\nPermease increases the permeability of the cell to β-galactosides.\nSo lactose from the medium enters the cell through the action of permease."},
{"id":"neet-2024-q140","pn":140,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","st":"Scientists and discoveries","q":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList I — List II\nA. Frederick Griffith — I. Genetic code\nB. Francois Jacob & Jacque Monod — II. Semi-conservative mode of DNA replication\nC. Har Gobind Khorana — III. Transformation\nD. Meselson & Stahl — IV. Lac operon\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below","o":["A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II"],"c":3,"s":"Frederick Griffith's experiments showed transformation in bacteria.\nFrancois Jacob and Jacques Monod worked out the lac operon.\nMeselson and Stahl showed the semi-conservative mode of DNA replication.\nHar Gobind Khorana developed a chemical method to define the base combinations of the genetic code.\nSo A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II."},
{"id":"neet-2024-q145","pn":145,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","st":"DNA replication","q":"Which of the following statement is correct regarding the process of replication in E.coli ?","o":["The DNA dependent DNA polymerase catalyses polymerization in 5'→3' as well as 3'→5' direction.","The DNA dependent DNA polymerase catalyses polymerization in 5'→3' direction.","The DNA dependent DNA polymerase catalyses polymerization in one direction, that is 3'→5'.","The DNA dependent RNA polymerase catalyase polymerization in one direction, that is 5'→3'."],"c":1,"s":"In prokaryotes such as E. coli, DNA dependent DNA polymerase catalyses polymerisation in only one direction, 5' → 3'."},
{"id":"neet-2023-q112","pn":112,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","st":"Hershey-Chase experiment","q":"Unequivocal proof that DNA is the genetic material was first proposed by","o":["Wilkins and Franklin","Frederick Griffith","Alfred Hershey and Martha Chase","Avery, Macleoid and McCarthy"],"c":2,"s":"The clear proof that DNA is the genetic material came from the Hershey and Chase experiment.\nAvery, MacLeod and McCarty did the biochemical characterisation of the transforming principle.\nGriffith did the transformation experiments on Pneumococcus.\nWilkins and Franklin produced X-ray diffraction data of DNA."},
{"id":"neet-2023-q117","pn":117,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","st":"Transcription: RNA polymerases","q":"What is the role of RNA polymerase III in the process of transcription in Eukaryotes ?","o":["Transcription of only snRNAs","Transcription of rRNA, (28 S, 18 S and 5.8 S)","Transcription of tRNA, 5 S rRNA and snRNA","Transcription of precursor of mRNA"],"c":2,"s":"Eukaryotes have three main RNA polymerases.\nRNA polymerase I makes 5.8S, 18S and 28S rRNAs.\nRNA polymerase II makes hnRNA, the precursor of mRNA.\nRNA polymerase III makes tRNAs, scRNA, 5S rRNA and snRNA."},
{"id":"neet-2023-q150","pn":150,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","st":"Ribosome composition","q":"How many different proteins does the ribosome consist of?","o":["20","80","60","40"],"c":1,"s":"The ribosome has structural RNAs and about 80 different proteins."},
{"id":"neet-2023-q159","pn":159,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","st":"Lac operon genes","q":"Match List I with List II.\nList I: A. Gene 'a'; B. Gene 'y'; C. Gene 'i'; D. Gene 'z'\nList II: I. β-galactosidase; II. Transacetylase; III. Permease; IV. Repressor protein\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","o":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II"],"c":2,"s":"In the lac operon:\nGene a codes for transacetylase.\nGene y codes for permease.\nGene i codes for the repressor protein.\nGene z codes for β-galactosidase."},
{"id":"neet-2023-q192","pn":192,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","st":"Transcription","q":"Which one of the following is the sequence on corresponding coding strand, if the sequence on mRNA formed is as follows 5′AUCGAUCGAUCGAUCGAUCGAUCGAUCG3′?","o":["3′ ATCGATCGATCGATCGATCGATCGATCG 5′","5′ UAGCUAGCUAGCUAGCUAGCUAGCUAGC 3′","3′ UAGCUAGCUAGCUAGCUAGCUAGCUAGC 5′","5′ ATCGATCGATCGATCGATCGATCGATCG 3′"],"c":3,"s":"The coding strand has the same sequence as mRNA, with T in place of U.\nTemplate strand: 3′-TAGCTAGCTAGCTAGCTAGCTAGCTAGC-5′.\nCoding strand: 5′-ATCGATCGATCGATCGATCGATCGATCG-3′.\nTranscription of the template gives the mRNA 5′-AUCG...-3′."},
{"id":"neet-2022-q118","pn":118,"y":"NEET 2022 · 17 Jul","tp":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","st":"Translation","q":"The process of translation of mRNA to proteins begins as soon as :","o":["The larger subunit of ribosome encounters mRNA","Both the subunits join together to bind with mRNA","The tRNA is activated and the larger subunit of ribosome encounters mRNA","The small subunit of ribosome encounters mRNA"],"c":3,"s":"Translation starts when the small ribosomal subunit meets the mRNA.\nThe larger subunit joins afterwards.\ntRNA is activated by amino acid attachment before this, in the first phase."},
{"id":"neet-2022-q123","pn":123,"y":"NEET 2022 · 17 Jul","tp":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","st":"DNA fingerprinting","q":"DNA polymorphism forms the basis of :","o":["DNA finger printing","Both genetic mapping and DNA finger printing","Translation","Genetic mapping"],"c":1,"s":"Polymorphism in DNA sequence is the basis of both genetic mapping and DNA fingerprinting."},
{"id":"neet-2022-q150","pn":150,"y":"NEET 2022 · 17 Jul","tp":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","st":"Human Genome Project methodology","q":"If a geneticist uses the blind approach for sequencing the whole genome of an organism, followed by assignment of function to different segments, the methodology adopted by him is called as :","o":["Gene mapping","Expressed sequence tags","Bioinformatics","Sequence annotation"],"c":3,"s":"Here the whole genome, coding and non-coding, is sequenced first.\nFunctions are then assigned to different regions.\nThis method is called sequence annotation."},
{"id":"neet-2022-q176","pn":176,"y":"NEET 2022 · 17 Jul","tp":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","st":"Lac operon","q":"In an E. Coll strain I gene gets mutated and tis product can not bind the inducer molecule. If growth medium is provided with lactose, what will be the outcome?","o":["z, y, a genes will be transcribed","z, y, a genes will not be translated","RNA polymerase will bind the promoter region","Only z gene will get transcribed"],"c":1,"s":"The product of the I gene is the repressor. It binds the operator and blocks transcription of z, y and a.\nInducer (lactose) normally attaches to the repressor and stops it binding the operator.\nHere the mutant repressor cannot bind the inducer, so it always stays on the operator.\nSo even with lactose, z, y and a are not transcribed, hence not translated."},
{"id":"neet-2022-q182","pn":182,"y":"NEET 2022 · 17 Jul","tp":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","st":"DNA packaging","q":"If the length of a DNA molecule is 1.1 metres, what will be the approximate number of base pairs?","o":["6.6 × 10⁹ bp","3.3 × 10⁶ bp","6.6 × 10⁶ bp","3.3 × 10⁹ bp"],"c":3,"s":"Number of base pairs × distance between two consecutive base pairs = length of DNA.\nDistance between base pairs is 0.34 nm = 0.34 × 10⁻⁹ m.\nNumber = 1.1 / (0.34 × 10⁻⁹) ≈ 3.3 × 10⁹ bp."},
{"id":"neet-2022-q188","pn":188,"y":"NEET 2022 · 17 Jul","tp":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","st":"Semi-conservative replication","q":"Ten E.coli cells with 15N – dsDNA are incubated in medium containing 14N nucleotide. After 60 minutes, how many E.coli cells will have DNA totally free from 15N?","o":["40 cells","60 cells","80 cells","20 cells"],"c":1,"s":"E. coli divides every 20 minutes, so 60 minutes means 3 generations.\n10 cells become 10 × 2³ = 80 cells.\nDNA replication is semi-conservative, so each original 15N strand stays in one cell; the 10 original cells have 20 old strands, so 20 cells carry 15N.\nCells fully free of 15N = 80 − 20 = 60."}
];
